WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.735
Thank you for joining us today.

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:02.869
Let's listen in.

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.004
This afternoon,

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:05.071
Rear Admiral John

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:06.740
Major will be providing an update

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.142
on the most recent findings from RV

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:10.844
operations in

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:12.879
search of the Titan submersible.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.481
He will provide a brief statement

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:15.749
and provide the opportunity

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:17.250
for questions after.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.818
Please limit your questions

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:20.420
to one per hour.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:21.554
Following the briefing,

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:22.555
the Joint Information

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:23.289
Center staff

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:24.958
and I will be here to help you

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:26.493
with any of your further needs.

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:27.260
May I now please

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.295
introduce Rear Admiral John McGraw

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:43.676
this morning.

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:46.413
And ROV, a remote operated vehicle

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.182
from the vessel Horizon Tech,

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:51.584
discovered the tail

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:54.120
cone of the Titan submersible

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:56.890
approximately 1600 feet

00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:59.859
from the bow of the Titanic

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.395
on the seafloor.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:05.265
The ROV subsequently

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:10.270
found additional debris in consultation

00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:12.138
with experts

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.240
from within the unified command.

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:17.010
The debris is consistent

00:01:17.444 --> 00:01:19.446
with the catastrophic loss

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:21.481
of the pressure chamber

00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:24.918
upon this determination.

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:28.088
We immediately notified the families

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:31.291
on behalf of the United States

00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:32.392
Coast Guard

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:35.095
and the entire unified command.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:38.465
I offer my deepest condolences

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:40.800
to the families

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:44.971
I can only imagine

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:48.174
what this has been like for them.

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:51.111
And I hope that this discovery

00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:53.613
provides some solace

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:57.450
during this difficult time.

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:04.524
Additionally, we've been in close contact

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:06.326
with the British

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:08.628
and French counsel general

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:10.830
to ensure that they are fully apprized

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:12.699
and that their concerns

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:14.767
are being addressed.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:17.036
The outpouring of support

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:20.773
in this highly complex search operation

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.642
has been robust

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.944
and immensely appreciated.

00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:25.945
We are grateful

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:27.547
for the rapid mobilization

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:30.350
and of experts on the undersea

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:31.818
search and rescue.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.853
And we thank all of the agencies

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:35.655
and personnel

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:38.825
for their role in the response.

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:40.860
We're also incredibly grateful

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:42.695
for the full spectrum

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:44.130
of international assistance

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.198
that's been provided.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:47.333
The ROV

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:48.134
will remain

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:49.002
on scene

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:51.271
and continue to gather information.

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:55.775
Again, our most heartfelt condolences

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.812
go out to the loved ones of the crew

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:05.552
Well, now take questions.

00:03:05.552 --> 00:03:08.621
What are we going to be?

00:03:08.655 --> 00:03:10.156
We're going to talk about

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:13.626
the delay in between the first

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:14.561
coming

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:19.065
This was a

00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:22.368
incredibly complex case,

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:25.338
and we're still working

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:29.175
to develop the details for the timeline

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:33.546
involved with this casualty

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.416
and the response.

00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:39.285
And so will provide that.

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:41.321
James Matthews from Sky News.

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:43.957
What other debris have you focused on?

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:47.360
Any trace of

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:50.230
So this is an incredibly complex

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:52.065
operating environment

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:54.334
on the seafloor over two miles

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:56.369
beneath the surface.

00:03:56.369 --> 00:03:59.038
And so the no remote operating

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:00.607
vehicle has been searching,

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:02.942
and it is highly capable.

00:04:02.942 --> 00:04:06.179
And we've been able to classify parts

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:08.581
of the pressure chamber

00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:11.084
for the Titan submersible.

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:13.119
Let me refer to

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:16.823
one of my undersea experts here Mr.

00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:20.126
Paul Hankin, to talk about the nature

00:04:20.126 --> 00:04:21.060
of some of the debris

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:27.934
Thank you, Admiral.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:30.637
So essentially, we found out

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:32.605
of five different

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:34.841
major pieces of debris that

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:38.911
told us that it was the

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.480
remains of the Titan.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.481
The initial thing we found

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:43.016
was a nose cone

00:04:43.016 --> 00:04:46.419
which was outside of the pressure hall.

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:48.955
We then found a large debris field

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:51.991
within that large debris field.

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:54.060
We found the

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.330
the front end bell of the pressure hole.

00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:02.001
That was the first indication that

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:05.071
there was a catastrophic event

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:07.874
Shortly thereafter, we found the

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:11.110
a second smaller debris field

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.046
within that debris field.

00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:14.347
We found

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.683
the the other end of the pressure hall

00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:19.752
to the aft and bell

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:22.221
which was basically that comprised

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:25.658
the totality of that pressure vessel.

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:28.861
We continue to map the debris field.

00:05:29.228 --> 00:05:31.097
And as the admiral said, we will

00:05:32.899 --> 00:05:35.068
do the best we can to

00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:37.770
fully map out what's down there.

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:40.373
Right now.

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:41.708
Thank you very much for that.

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:42.542
It's a it's

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:44.110
a very difficult question to ask,

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:45.578
but it will be an important one

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:46.713
for the families, of course.

00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:48.915
But what are the prospects of recovery

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:54.287
So so the questions arise related.

00:05:54.287 --> 00:05:55.455
I'm restating the question

00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:56.389
from the standpoint

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:57.423
of sometimes it's

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:59.125
hard to hear the question here.

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:01.160
What are the prospects for

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:03.429
recovering crew members?

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:06.766
And so this is a incredibly unforgiving

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:09.669
environment down there on the sea floor.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:12.872
And the debris is consistent

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:14.941
with a catastrophic

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:17.410
implosion of the vessel.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.047
And so we'll continue to work

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:24.083
and continue to search the area

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:25.385
down there.

00:06:25.385 --> 00:06:29.722
But I don't have an answer for prospects

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:30.490
at this time.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:32.425
Every time a fellow at NBC News

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:35.495
is being sorry.

00:06:35.495 --> 00:06:36.929
Any suggestion at all

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:38.865
that the at the shop itself

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:40.967
collided with the wreckage of Titanic

00:06:40.967 --> 00:06:42.235
or that instead

00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:44.237
it might have imploded above the wreckage

00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:46.773
and then rained down nearby?

00:06:46.773 --> 00:06:48.341
So the question was,

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:49.876
is there any question as to

00:06:49.876 --> 00:06:50.376
whether or not

00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:52.111
the sub collided with the Titanic

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:55.214
or whether it imploded above

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:58.184
and debris field created from that.

00:06:58.951 --> 00:07:02.922
So the the the location of the Titan

00:07:02.922 --> 00:07:05.158
submersible was in an area

00:07:05.158 --> 00:07:07.260
that was approximately 600 feet

00:07:07.894 --> 00:07:11.497
from the wreck of the Titanic.

00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:14.967
I have an expert here that can

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:16.002
that is familiar

00:07:16.002 --> 00:07:18.571
with that area and can talk about

00:07:18.571 --> 00:07:19.806
the debris field

00:07:19.806 --> 00:07:21.374
and what

00:07:21.374 --> 00:07:22.175
the debris field

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:24.744
indicates in terms of the

00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:27.146
where the casualty may have occurred

00:07:27.146 --> 00:07:28.347
relatively quickly.

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:29.415
Karl, tell me when that

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.285
was called back on.

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:33.419
The expert voice is coming up

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:34.620
to answer the question.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:35.922
Thank you, Admiral.

00:07:35.922 --> 00:07:36.989
So the question is,

00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:38.391
where does the wreck lie

00:07:38.391 --> 00:07:40.493
in relation to the Titanic?

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.929
I didn't hear the admiral's answer,

00:07:42.929 --> 00:07:44.497
I think. 600 feet.

00:07:44.497 --> 00:07:46.299
Was that correct, Admiral?

00:07:46.299 --> 00:07:49.135
So that's that's off the bow of Titanic.

00:07:49.368 --> 00:07:50.303
It's in an area

00:07:50.303 --> 00:07:53.473
where there is not any debris of Titanic.

00:07:53.506 --> 00:07:56.108
It is a smooth bottom there.

00:07:56.342 --> 00:07:57.577
To my knowledge, in anything

00:07:57.577 --> 00:07:59.679
I've seen, there's no Titanic wreckage

00:07:59.679 --> 00:08:00.713
in that area.

00:08:00.713 --> 00:08:03.583
And again, 200 plus meters from the bow.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:05.084
And consistent

00:08:05.084 --> 00:08:07.353
with the location of last communication

00:08:07.987 --> 00:08:10.022
for an implosion in the water column

00:08:10.022 --> 00:08:12.058
and the size of the debris field

00:08:12.058 --> 00:08:13.292
is consistent

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:15.328
with that implosion in the water column.

00:08:16.462 --> 00:08:16.562
Well,

00:08:16.562 --> 00:08:17.396
there are

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:18.231
some question

00:08:18.231 --> 00:08:20.299
in terms of the timing here.

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:21.267
You say that

00:08:21.267 --> 00:08:23.603
this was a catastrophic explosion

00:08:23.603 --> 00:08:24.570
and I know it's early on,

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:25.805
but is it your estimation

00:08:25.805 --> 00:08:27.139
that this happened

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:28.140
right at the moment

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:29.675
when they lost contact

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:31.210
an hour and 45 minutes after?

00:08:32.645 --> 00:08:32.912
So the

00:08:32.912 --> 00:08:33.412
question was

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:33.813
about the

00:08:33.813 --> 00:08:36.215
timing of the catastrophic implosion

00:08:36.883 --> 00:08:37.617
Right now,

00:08:37.617 --> 00:08:41.254
it is too early to tell with that.

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:44.924
We know that as we've been prosecuting

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:46.292
this search

00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:50.496
over the course of the last 72 hours,

00:08:51.063 --> 00:08:52.865
and beyond

00:08:52.865 --> 00:08:55.334
that, we've had sonar buoys in the water

00:08:55.501 --> 00:08:58.905
nearly continuously and have not detected

00:08:58.905 --> 00:09:01.040
any catastrophic events

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:02.508
when those sonobuoys

00:09:02.508 --> 00:09:03.809
have been in the water.

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:05.711
So how would you describe

00:09:05.711 --> 00:09:07.346
what happens from here, sir,

00:09:07.346 --> 00:09:10.583
in the next days and weeks?

00:09:13.519 --> 00:09:14.420
We will.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:15.788
That question was,

00:09:15.788 --> 00:09:17.757
what happens from here? What?

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:18.624
What's the next?

00:09:18.624 --> 00:09:21.661
Our thoughts are with the families

00:09:22.495 --> 00:09:25.197
and making sure that

00:09:25.197 --> 00:09:27.166
they have

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:29.535
an understanding

00:09:29.535 --> 00:09:33.039
as best as we can provide

00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:36.375
of of what happened and begin

00:09:36.375 --> 00:09:39.245
to find some closure

00:09:40.112 --> 00:09:41.881
in terms of the large process.

00:09:41.881 --> 00:09:43.849
We're going to continue to

00:09:45.217 --> 00:09:47.153
investigate the

00:09:47.153 --> 00:09:49.555
site of the debris field.

00:09:50.423 --> 00:09:51.524
And then I know that there's

00:09:51.524 --> 00:09:55.194
also a lot of questions about how, why

00:09:55.227 --> 00:09:57.330
and when did this happen.

00:09:58.464 --> 00:10:02.234
And so, you know, those are questions

00:10:02.234 --> 00:10:04.904
that we will collect

00:10:04.904 --> 00:10:08.040
as much information as we can on Now,

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:11.978
while the governments are meeting

00:10:11.978 --> 00:10:15.915
and discussing, you know, what

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:20.119
an investigation of this nature

00:10:20.119 --> 00:10:22.288
of a casualty might look like,

00:10:22.722 --> 00:10:24.090
this is something that happened.

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:25.524
I'll just just remind everybody,

00:10:25.524 --> 00:10:26.659
this is something that happened

00:10:26.659 --> 00:10:30.763
in a remote portion of the ocean with

00:10:32.064 --> 00:10:35.034
people from

00:10:35.034 --> 00:10:36.202
several different countries

00:10:36.202 --> 00:10:37.703
around the world.

00:10:37.703 --> 00:10:40.439
And so it is a complex case

00:10:40.439 --> 00:10:41.040
to work through,

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.709
but I'm confident that

00:10:43.709 --> 00:10:45.378
those questions will begin

00:10:45.378 --> 00:10:46.646
to get answered

00:10:50.683 --> 00:10:51.550
in the U.K..

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:53.185
Is there any suggestion

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:55.321
this time factors mean

00:10:55.321 --> 00:10:57.156
anything could have prevented this

00:10:57.156 --> 00:10:59.425
to save the five people on board, or

00:10:59.425 --> 00:11:00.359
is this quite simply

00:11:02.361 --> 00:11:03.195
the.

00:11:03.195 --> 00:11:04.764
The question was,

00:11:04.764 --> 00:11:05.931
was there any suggestion

00:11:05.931 --> 00:11:08.534
that time factors

00:11:08.534 --> 00:11:11.170
may have played a

00:11:13.072 --> 00:11:14.640
role or

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:17.743
consideration in the casualty here?

00:11:18.177 --> 00:11:19.545
And so the debris field

00:11:19.545 --> 00:11:22.415
is consistent with a catastrophic

00:11:22.415 --> 00:11:25.084
implosion of the vessel.

00:11:25.084 --> 00:11:27.153
Again, while we were prosecuting

00:11:27.153 --> 00:11:27.586
the search,

00:11:27.586 --> 00:11:28.487
we had

00:11:28.487 --> 00:11:31.123
listening devices in the water throughout

00:11:31.357 --> 00:11:34.527
and did not hear any signs

00:11:34.527 --> 00:11:38.064
of catastrophic failure from those.

00:11:38.097 --> 00:11:38.698
And so we're going to

00:11:38.698 --> 00:11:41.167
continue to investigate

00:11:42.034 --> 00:11:44.537
or we're going to continue to document

00:11:44.537 --> 00:11:45.604
the information there.

00:11:45.604 --> 00:11:47.640
And and understand

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:48.841
based on all the information

00:11:48.841 --> 00:11:51.110
we have, the timeline,

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:54.080
not how many in the investigation.

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:56.348
So what does it take for the resources

00:11:56.549 --> 00:11:58.150
required for the investigation,

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:00.853
which is going out in

00:12:01.454 --> 00:12:03.022
So the question was what

00:12:03.022 --> 00:12:04.323
what are the resources

00:12:04.323 --> 00:12:05.591
required for the investigation

00:12:05.591 --> 00:12:07.126
in which ships will be pulling out

00:12:07.126 --> 00:12:08.894
and staying in?

00:12:08.894 --> 00:12:12.431
And so it's too early for me

00:12:12.431 --> 00:12:14.366
to talk about an investigation.

00:12:14.366 --> 00:12:15.267
That's a decision

00:12:15.267 --> 00:12:17.269
that's going to be taken outside of

00:12:17.903 --> 00:12:21.140
the search and and efforts

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:23.275
that I was leading.

00:12:23.275 --> 00:12:26.011
And but we do have a number of vessels.

00:12:26.011 --> 00:12:27.747
We have nine vessels on the scene

00:12:27.747 --> 00:12:28.748
right now.

00:12:28.748 --> 00:12:31.250
We had medical personnel on scene.

00:12:31.283 --> 00:12:33.486
We had other technicians on scene.

00:12:33.753 --> 00:12:37.323
And so we will begin to demobilize

00:12:37.823 --> 00:12:41.393
personnel and vessels from the scene

00:12:41.961 --> 00:12:45.531
over the course of the next 24 hours.

00:12:46.065 --> 00:12:48.267
But we're going to continue

00:12:48.267 --> 00:12:51.070
remote operations on the sea floor.

00:12:51.871 --> 00:12:54.907
And and I don't have a timeline for when

00:12:55.207 --> 00:12:58.210
we would intend to stop remote operations

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.012
on the sea floor at this point.

00:13:00.012 --> 00:13:01.514
Admiral, as a result of this,

00:13:02.782 --> 00:13:03.415
as a result

00:13:03.415 --> 00:13:03.916
of this, do you

00:13:03.916 --> 00:13:04.350
think there

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:06.452
should be changes in the way

00:13:06.452 --> 00:13:09.588
these are safely rated or inspected

00:13:09.588 --> 00:13:11.390
so that this won't happen again?

00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:11.924
Yeah.

00:13:11.924 --> 00:13:13.692
The question was essentially about

00:13:13.692 --> 00:13:15.060
do you think that there should be changes

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:17.096
in safety ratings or inspection

00:13:17.096 --> 00:13:19.865
for these standards at

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:22.701
I know that there's a lot of questions

00:13:22.701 --> 00:13:23.969
about why, how,

00:13:23.969 --> 00:13:25.805
when this happened

00:13:25.805 --> 00:13:28.474
and the members of the unified command,

00:13:28.474 --> 00:13:29.308
you know,

00:13:29.308 --> 00:13:30.643
have those questions

00:13:30.643 --> 00:13:31.277
to ask

00:13:31.277 --> 00:13:34.113
as professionals and experts that work

00:13:34.513 --> 00:13:35.447
in this environment.

00:13:35.447 --> 00:13:38.918
And this is an incredibly difficult

00:13:38.918 --> 00:13:40.953
and dangerous environment

00:13:40.953 --> 00:13:43.055
to work in out there.

00:13:43.055 --> 00:13:45.925
But those those questions about

00:13:46.692 --> 00:13:49.895
the regulations that apply and

00:13:50.496 --> 00:13:51.564
the standards,

00:13:51.564 --> 00:13:53.632
that's going to be, I'm sure,

00:13:54.166 --> 00:13:56.702
focus on future review.

00:13:57.303 --> 00:14:01.140
Right now, we're focused on documenting

00:14:02.041 --> 00:14:05.010
the the scene and continuing the

00:14:06.011 --> 00:14:07.980
we are trying to

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:10.115
find out there are we

00:14:10.115 --> 00:14:11.350
we don't know the timing here,

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:12.618
but there was the banging noises

00:14:12.618 --> 00:14:14.420
yesterday that redirected

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:16.655
and we deployed the ROV to this area.

00:14:16.856 --> 00:14:17.423
Is there anything

00:14:17.423 --> 00:14:18.958
conclusive in those noises?

00:14:18.958 --> 00:14:20.693
And to that redirection?

00:14:20.693 --> 00:14:23.195
And then finding this debris field today

00:14:23.863 --> 00:14:24.997
all help.

00:14:25.364 --> 00:14:28.434
So throughout the search efforts,

00:14:28.434 --> 00:14:32.371
we reacted to the information

00:14:32.771 --> 00:14:35.507
that we had available to us.

00:14:35.507 --> 00:14:38.811
And while we continue to send it off for

00:14:39.411 --> 00:14:42.114
deeper analysis, again, really complex

00:14:42.348 --> 00:14:44.783
operating environment for us to work in.

00:14:46.552 --> 00:14:49.021
Let me check with the experts.

00:14:49.021 --> 00:14:51.490
But there doesn't appear to be any

00:14:52.024 --> 00:14:55.194
connection between the noises and

00:14:56.328 --> 00:14:58.797
the location on the seafloor.

00:14:58.831 --> 00:14:59.832
Again,

00:15:00.099 --> 00:15:03.469
this was a catastrophic

00:15:03.736 --> 00:15:05.337
implosion of the vessel,

00:15:05.337 --> 00:15:07.239
which would have generated

00:15:07.239 --> 00:15:09.441
a significant broadband sound

00:15:10.409 --> 00:15:11.076
down there

00:15:11.076 --> 00:15:13.345
that the sonobuoys for it have picked up.

00:15:14.313 --> 00:15:17.149
This will be the last question

00:15:17.583 --> 00:15:19.685
I'm going to ask you about

00:15:19.685 --> 00:15:20.653
regarding the family

00:15:20.653 --> 00:15:21.420
that dropped

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:23.322
into the ocean today and arrived early,

00:15:23.322 --> 00:15:24.590
early this morning at sea,

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:26.759
swiftly was able to find the debris field

00:15:26.759 --> 00:15:28.027
by the speed of that.

00:15:28.027 --> 00:15:30.663
And also were all of the assets involved

00:15:30.996 --> 00:15:33.699
moved as swiftly as possible to the area?

00:15:34.233 --> 00:15:35.935
Was any help turned away?

00:15:35.935 --> 00:15:37.603
Some lawmakers

00:15:38.971 --> 00:15:43.275
This was a incredibly complex operation

00:15:43.943 --> 00:15:47.746
and we were able to mobilize

00:15:47.746 --> 00:15:51.650
an immense amount of gear to the site in

00:15:52.151 --> 00:15:55.688
just a really remarkable amount of time.

00:15:56.388 --> 00:15:58.624
Given the fact that we started

00:15:58.624 --> 00:16:01.627
without any sort of vessel response

00:16:01.627 --> 00:16:02.861
plan for this

00:16:02.861 --> 00:16:05.464
or any sort of pre-staged resources

00:16:05.898 --> 00:16:07.533
and so the equipment

00:16:07.533 --> 00:16:09.702
that was brought on site this morning

00:16:09.902 --> 00:16:14.440
that we were using was a pelagic ROV,

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:17.142
capable of operating at 6000 meters.

00:16:17.643 --> 00:16:19.945
Camera sonar, other

00:16:20.479 --> 00:16:22.982
articulating arms and resources on it.

00:16:23.749 --> 00:16:25.985
And it you know,

00:16:25.985 --> 00:16:28.821
we had to transport it here through C-17

00:16:29.021 --> 00:16:29.455
aircraft.

00:16:29.455 --> 00:16:30.522
This is two aircraft

00:16:30.522 --> 00:16:32.324
that it took to get this up here.

00:16:32.324 --> 00:16:36.428
And so we've really had the right gear

00:16:36.428 --> 00:16:38.330
onsite and worked

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:42.101
as us as as swiftly as possible

00:16:42.101 --> 00:16:44.103
to bring all of the capabilities

00:16:44.103 --> 00:16:46.138
that we had to bear

00:16:46.138 --> 00:16:48.340
to this search and rescue effort.

00:16:49.041 --> 00:16:51.677
And it was just a huge international

00:16:51.677 --> 00:16:53.312
and interagency

00:16:53.312 --> 00:16:55.147
effort to make this happen.

00:16:55.147 --> 00:16:58.951
So I'm really grateful for all of

00:16:59.218 --> 00:17:00.552
the responders

00:17:00.552 --> 00:17:02.621
that came out to support this.

00:17:03.322 --> 00:17:05.057
And and really

00:17:06.158 --> 00:17:09.028
search for

00:17:09.028 --> 00:17:09.795
for the vessel.

00:17:09.795 --> 00:17:12.564
It is a difficult day for all of us.

00:17:13.799 --> 00:17:15.901
And it's especially difficult

00:17:16.268 --> 00:17:17.136
for the families.

00:17:17.136 --> 00:17:19.738
And our thoughts are with the families

00:17:19.738 --> 00:17:21.106
today.

00:17:21.306 --> 00:17:24.076
But this was an immense support.

00:17:24.076 --> 00:17:26.311
And we had the right gear on the bottom

00:17:26.311 --> 00:17:28.514
to to find it

00:17:29.548 --> 00:17:31.150
not making it.

00:17:31.216 --> 00:17:32.985
Will you attempt to recover the bodies?

00:17:32.985 --> 00:17:34.253
Can we ask you about the victims

00:17:34.253 --> 00:17:35.187
who may be recovering?

00:17:35.187 --> 00:17:36.355
Thank you so much, everyone,

00:17:36.355 --> 00:17:38.157
for attending this afternoon.

00:17:38.157 --> 00:17:39.024
There are no future

00:17:39.024 --> 00:17:40.592
